===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|uggen}}, from {{der|en|non|ugga||to fear}}, see above.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|ugg}}, {{l|en|oug}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|North England|and|Scotland|obsolete}} To [[dread]], [[loathe]] or [[disgust]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1822
|title=Poems, Chiefly in the Scottish Dialect
|first=Robert|last=Wilson
|chapter=Answer to an Epistle from a Friend
|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=iJxgAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA71
|page=71
|passage=Wha weds a cankert thriftless wife, / Weds to his days eternal strife, / For, like the Tron-Kirk bell, / She ever hammers on his lugs, / Till her an' hame at last he '''uggs''' / As the dire door o' hell!
}}
# {{lb|en|North England|and|Scotland|obsolete}} To fear, be [[horrified]]; [[shudder]] with horror.
# {{lb|en|North England|and|Scotland|obsolete}} To [[vomit]].
# {{lb|en|Northumbria|obsolete}} To give a [[surfeit]] to.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|feel abhorrence}} {{l|en|abhor}}, {{l|en|loathe}}; see also [[Thesaurus:hate]].  Alternatively: {{l|en|nauseate}}, {{l|en|sicken}}.
* {{sense|vomit}} {{l|en|heave}}, {{l|en|pray to the porcelain god}}, {{l|en|throw up}}; see also [[Thesaurus:regurgitate]]

